  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and mild conditions expected to continue \nthrough Saturday.\n\nPer the 06Z surface analysis, high pressure remains sprawled\nacross the Ohio Valley into the Delmarva Peninsula. This is\nsupporting another seasonably chilly start to the morning. Based\non 08Z/3 AM temperatures, many have plunged well into the 30s\nwith spotty upper 20s in more rural locations. At the same time,\nhigher elevations sitting above the dome of cold air are sitting\nin the 40s. This has led to rather sharp gradients a few hours \nahead of daybreak. Relatively light winds should persist with \nany passing high clouds likely confined to along/east of I-95.\n\nLike much of this week, sunshine will again be abundant with\nhigh pressure in charge one more day. 850-mb temperatures rise\nto around 8C which carries forecast highs to around the 70 \ndegree mark. Gradients remain weak which supports light and\nvariable winds through the day. Heading into the tonight, expect\nclouds to increase from south to north given the approach from\nlow pressure from the southeastern U.S. This yields a bump up in\nlow temperatures will most in the mid 30s to low 40s.\n\nThe start of the weekend carries one more dry day, but with\nincreased cloud cover. High pressure gradually retreats into New\nEngland while an inverted trough along the coast approaches from\nthe south. The shift in the surface pattern favors easterly\nonshore gradients. Forecast highs drop off by around 2 to 5\ndegrees, but this is still above average for the time of year.\nAny potential for rain holds off until late Saturday night, if\nnot a bit longer. Nighttime lows are set to fall into the 40s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Forecast remains uncertain as a coastal low tracks \nup the Eastern Seaboard Sunday into Monday, followed by a cool\ndown.\n\nAn area of low pressure is forecast to develop off the Southeast \nU.S. coast late this week, then track northward along the coast this \nweekend. The low's initial forward speed is going to be slow as it \ngets trapped underneath a brief, weak upper ridge, then quickly \npicks up speed as it moves northeast with a strong upper trough \napproaching from the OH Valley. There remains considerable \ndifferences among the global guidance, both run to run and between \nmodels in each run. The ensemble guidance for both the European and \nGFS remain quite dispersive with low tracks well inland across the \nNC/VA Piedmont to well offshore of the Mid-Atlantic. \n\nThere continues to be too much uncertainty to pin down any specific \ndetails or impacts to our area. Rain chances Sunday into Sunday \nnight continue to slowly increase, especially east of the Blue Ridge \nwith the highest certainty east of I-95. The exact track of the low \nis going to be critical to where the heaviest rain falls, though \nthere could be a sharp gradient on the western side of the precip \nshield from heavy to very light rain amounts. It is worth mentioning \nthe higher-end of the ensemble spread indicates 2-4\" as a worst-case \nscenario, but many members also have little to no rain for most of \nthe area - this probably speak the best to how much uncertainty \nremains. The location of the heaviest QPF also varies greatly from \nalong US-15 to the Delmarva. Continue to monitor the forecast over \nthe next few days as guidance hopefully starts to converge on a \nsolution for this weekend. \n\nA strong Canadian High builds across much of the central to eastern \nCONUS during the early part of next week. This is going to bring us \ndry, brisk, and much colder conditions. \n\nAnother trough is progged to move across the Ohio Valley Wednesday \nthrough Friday next week. Depending on your model or ensemble of \nchoice, a low may develop and bring widespread precip to the area. \nColder solutions do favor snow in the mountains of the\nAlleghenies.",
